% ============================================================================
% heat-transfer — the three modes of thermal-energy transfer (conduction,
% convection, radiation) and HOW / through-what each one moves heat, in the
% source's own words (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of thermodynamics, and a rung a PHYSICS or an
% EARTH-SCIENCE agent reasons UP from: heat (thermal energy) only ever moves
% from a warmer thing to a cooler thing, and it can do so in exactly THREE
% ways. "Conduction moves heat by direct contact; convection moves heat by the
% motion of gasses and liquids; radiation moves heat by light waves." Before
% you can reason about a stove, a weather system, or a spacecraft's thermal
% control you must first know WHICH of the three modes is carrying the heat.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? heat_transfer_mode(conduction, $Mechanism)      % direct_contact
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `heat_transfer_mode(mode, mechanism)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the mechanism WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on a word that is not one of these three modes (it never invents a mechanism).
%
% Each of the three modes and the mechanism the source states it transfers heat
% by, as a little truth table:
%
%     mode          how / through-what it transfers heat   a beginner's cue
%     ----------    ------------------------------------   -----------------------
%     conduction    direct_contact                         a hot spoon warms your hand
%     convection    motion_of_gasses_and_liquids           warm air rises, cool air sinks
%     radiation     light_waves                            the Sun warms you across space
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a mechanism and recall its mode:
%
%     ? heat_transfer_mode($Mode, light_waves)   % radiation
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three modes — `evaporation`, `advection`,
% `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a heat-transfer recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a mechanism. That honest-abstention property is what
% makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% mode->mechanism pairs it can ground, and only those. (Evaporation is a
% phase change, not one of the three modes of heat transfer — so it abstains.)
%
% The `mechanism` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be
% the mechanism the source EXPLICITLY states for that mode — never one the
% source does not support. Where the token could have used an everyday word,
% it uses the source's OWN word instead (the source says the "motion of gasses
% and liquids", not "fluid flow"; it says "light waves", not "electromagnetic
% waves"), so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every mode->mechanism pair
% below was read out of a fetched NASA "Thermal Energy Transfer" Educator Notes
% document, and any pair whose mechanism could not be verified there would have
% been dropped). Each pair, with the primary source it was copied from and the
% verbatim span that states it:
%
%   conduction -> direct_contact
%     https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/thermal-energy-transfer-classroom-connection-final-v508.pdf
%     (NASA Next Gen STEM — Thermal Energy Transfer, Educator Notes, p.2)
%     "Conduction - the transfer of thermal energy by coming in direct contact
%      with another object (e.g., holding a cup of hot chocolate, walking bare
%      foot across a cold floor)"
%
%   convection -> motion_of_gasses_and_liquids
%     https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/thermal-energy-transfer-classroom-connection-final-v508.pdf
%     (NASA Next Gen STEM — Thermal Energy Transfer, Educator Notes, p.2)
%     "Convection - the motion of gasses and liquids due to different densities
%      (e.g., when warm air rises in your home)"
%
%   radiation -> light_waves
%     https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/thermal-energy-transfer-classroom-connection-final-v508.pdf
%     (NASA Next Gen STEM — Thermal Energy Transfer, Educator Notes, p.2)
%     "Radiation - the absorption and emitting of visible or invisible light
%      waves, called radiation, to from one object to another (e.g., sunlight
%      on your skin, heating food in a microwave)"
%
% (The three-way framing itself is stated on the same page: "Thermal energy can
% be transferred three ways:" followed by conduction, convection, and radiation
% — another span of the same primary NASA teaching resource.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the NASA statement that fixes the
% first row (conduction -> direct_contact). Every OTHER row's per-fact source
% and verbatim span is listed above, so each mechanism remains independently
% checkable. All sources are NASA Next Gen STEM educator notes, a primary U.S.
% government (National Aeronautics and Space Administration) teaching resource,
% so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table heat_transfer_mode {
    columns mode, mechanism

    row (conduction, direct_contact)
    row (convection, motion_of_gasses_and_liquids)
    row (radiation, light_waves)

    source "Conduction - the transfer of thermal energy by coming in direct contact with another object (e.g., holding a cup of hot chocolate, walking bare foot across a cold floor)"
    locator "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/thermal-energy-transfer-classroom-connection-final-v508.pdf"
    trust authoritative
}
